**Definition 1.5 (The supplied no-worse and event correspondences).**
For an original channel write
$\widehat T(X)=D^\dagger T(DXD^\dagger)D$ and
$Q_J(t)=O(t)^T\Pi_JO(t)$. The following are credited suppliers:
QMB Theorem 2.1, QPC Theorem 3.3, and QRC Theorems 2.2, 3.2 and 6.2.
For every original complex $T\in\mathfrak C_1$ there is $x\in\mathcal F$
such that the physical channel
$T_x(X)=DS_x(D^\dagger XD)D^\dagger$ has $e(T_x)\le e(T)$.
It has a fixed scalar realization with at most $225$ flags. For every
$x\in\mathcal F$, put

$$
\begin{gathered}
\mathcal J_{15}=\{J\subseteq\mathsf S:1\le|J|\le2\},\qquad
t_J=(\operatorname{tr}(H_i\Pi_J))_i,\\
H_J(x)=\Pi_J-S_x^\dagger(\Pi_J)
=\sum_i[t_J-M^Tt_J]_iH_i
 +\sum_\nu(1-\eta_\nu)P_\nu(\Pi_J),\\
e(T_x)=\max_{J\in\mathcal J_{15}}\|H_J(x)\|_{\rm op}.
\end{gathered}
\tag{1.11}
$$

The adjoint, and hence $M^T$, is essential in this formula. It is
not replaced by the state map. All five singleton and ten pair
events belong to this same channel and this same marginal.

The real replacement in QRC can change a channel. If a rank-one
complex factor is $s\,ab^\dagger$, write $a=u+iv$, $b=w+iz$.
QRC uses the four real rank-at-most-one factors
$s\,u w^T,s\,u z^T,s\,v w^T,s\,v z^T$ in a new channel.
The summed squared-adjoint factors are the real part of the original
TP normalization, hence still $I$. Direct matrix sandwiches prove
complete positivity of this new composite; transposition itself is
not assumed CP. Realification contracts every real symmetric event
residual simultaneously. Walk stationarity and the specified reflection
are then obtained by the credited no-worse averaging. Thus (1.11)
does not assume real Kraus factors for an unchanged complex channel,
full $O(5)$ menu invariance, or a definite-character rank-one Kraus
decomposition. The counterexamples in QPC Propositions 9.1–9.3
remain applicable to those stronger claims.

